# =============== axes (2) — what our silence means ===============

<epr:registry:axis:epistemic> a <epr:vocab#Axis> ;
  skos:prefLabel "epistemic" ;
  dct:description "Communal standing of a claim — how emergent truth moves through peer review toward settled canon. This axis is a standing verdict derived at the evidence-holding edge (a deterministic fold over witnessed REA review events), never a stored status column, and it is explained by a witness (the checks/observations folded, not an opaque score). Every contest point routes to `refer`, never to silence: an active dispute is a first-class outcome of the classification, not an error state or a stale value." ;
  <epr:vocab#vocabulary> <epr:schema:enum:epistemic-status> ;
  <epr:vocab#kind> "derived" ;
  <epr:vocab#factClosure> "open" ;
  <epr:vocab#closureRationale> "FACTS are open because a claim nobody has reviewed is unreviewed, not rejected — silence from a peer is a partial view, never a Dismiss. The shipped code already behaves this way: `standing_ratio()` returns `Option<f64>` and yields `None` on zero evidence rather than 0.0, so 'no reviews' can never be read as 'reviewed badly', and classification of an unreviewed claim is `emergent` — served honestly as emergent, never inflated and never treated as refuted. VERDICTS are closed because `cite_gate` gates an action: a claim that has not earned standing does not cite, and the absence of accumulated affirmation is a refusal to promote, not a licence to proceed while we wait. The two postures meet at `classify()`, which is total, and the contested case routes to `refer` rather than resolving silently to either side." ;
  <epr:vocab#orthogonalTo> "witness-ladder (evidentiary custody of the record: local -> peer-validated -> notarized; README.md:208)" ;
  <epr:vocab#orthogonalTo> "epr:registry:axis:reach (visibility/disclosure — a different question about the same claim)" ;
  <epr:vocab#verdictClosure> "closed" .

<epr:registry:axis:reach> a <epr:vocab#Axis> ;
  skos:prefLabel "reach" ;
  dct:description "Disclosure of a claim across the SOCIAL axis — who may see this, now. Two layers, per the vocabulary-split spec §2: a DECLARED FLOOR authored at creation (the eight-value reach vocabulary, notarized in the content_store_integrity zome) and a DERIVED VERDICT that may narrow it at serve time based on the author's earned standing. Composition is narrowing-only: the derived layer can refuse or withhold reach the floor declared, and can never widen past it. Reach is NOT epistemic standing (how well-reviewed a claim is), NOT the witness ladder (evidentiary custody of the record), and NOT retention (disclosure across the TEMPORAL axis — what may still be seen later). A well-reviewed claim may be private; an unreviewed claim may be public. Confusing reach with standing is the drift this card exists to resist." ;
  <epr:vocab#vocabulary> <epr:schema:enum:reach> ;
  <epr:vocab#kind> "two-layer" ;
  <epr:vocab#factClosure> "open" ;
  <epr:vocab#closureRationale> "FACTS are open because standing evidence is gathered from a partial view of the network: not having observed an author's contributions is not evidence that they have none, which is exactly why `Standing::Unknown` is a distinct value rather than a zero, and why the registry carries an explicit `UnknownTreatment` policy instead of silently reading absence as bad standing. VERDICTS are closed because reach gates disclosure, and disclosure is irreversible — bytes served cannot be unserved. The absence of earned standing is a refusal to widen, never an 'unknown, therefore serve it and find out'. This is the fail-open inversion the OWL2 graduation refused at the verdict layer: under an open-world reading, a missing standing assertion would read as 'possibly sufficient', and an adversary's optimal move becomes withholding evidence." ;
  <epr:vocab#orthogonalTo> "epr:registry:axis:epistemic (communal standing of the claim — how reviewed, not how visible)" ;
  <epr:vocab#orthogonalTo> "witness-ladder (evidentiary custody of the record: local -> peer-validated -> notarized)" ;
  <epr:vocab#orthogonalTo> "retention (the temporal half of the same disclosure gradient — not yet carded)" ;
  <epr:vocab#verdictClosure> "closed" .

# 41 vocabularies · 315 concepts · 2 axes
